{{Politics of Okaiken}}

The {{nihongo|'''House of Commoners'''|衆議院|Shūgiin}} is the [[lower house]] of the [[National Diet]] of [[Okaiken]]. The [[House of Officers]] is the [[upper house]].

The House of Commoners has 475 members, elected for a four-year term. The last general election was held on October 27, 2025. The house is the more powerful of the two houses, able to override vetoes on bills imposed by the House of Officers with a two-thirds majority. It can be dissolved by the [[Prime Minister of Okaiken|Prime Minister]] at will. The most recent dissolution was by Prime Minister [[Sho Aoyagi]] on June 10, 2021.

== Powers ==
The House of Commoners is the more powerful chamber of the Diet, possessing powers that are not granted to the House of Officers.

* '''Legislation:''' If a bill is passed by the House of Commoners but is voted down, amended, or not acted upon within 60 days by the House of Officers, the bill is returned to the House of Commoners. The Commoners can then pass the bill into law by securing a two-thirds majority of those present.
* '''Budget and Treaties:''' The budget and the ratification of international treaties must be introduced in the House of Commoners first. If the House of Officers disagrees with the House of Commoners, the decision of the Commoners becomes the decision of the Diet after 30 days, or immediately if a joint committee fails to reach an agreement.
* '''Designation of the Prime Minister:''' The House of Commoners has the final say in appointing the Prime Minister. If the two houses cannot agree on a candidate, the choice of the House of Commoners prevails.
* '''Confidence and No-Confidence:''' The House of Commoners is the only chamber that can hold the government to account via a motion of no-confidence. If a no-confidence motion is passed, the Cabinet must resign ''en masse'', or the Prime Minister must dissolve the House of Commoners within ten days, triggering a general election.

== History ==
=== Post-war LDP dominance ===
For most of the late 20th century, Okaiken politics was dominated by the [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Liberal Democratic Party (LDP)]], which held a near-unbroken streak in government from 1972. This period was marked by rapid economic growth but also by political stagnation.

=== 2016 Election: The Red Reckoning ===
The LDP's dominance was shattered in the [[Okaiken general election, 2016|2016 general election]]. Wracked by corruption scandals and a damaging internal leadership crisis, the LDP collapsed to third place. The [[Sanese Communist Party|Sanese Communist Party (SCP)]], led by [[Sho Aoyagi]], won a historic plurality, forming a "left-wing" coalition with the [[Progressive Party of Okaiken|Progressive Party of Okaiken (PPO)]] and [[Honda Shigetsuma]].

=== 2021 Election: The Progressive Pivot ===
The Aoyagi government (2016–2021) was turbulent, plagued by high inflation and coalition infighting. In the [[Okaiken general election, 2021|2021 general election]], the PPO, led by Honda, surged to 201 seats, becoming the largest party. The SCP collapsed, and Honda formed a PPO-led minority government.

=== 59th Diet (2021–2025) ===
The 59th Diet was led by Prime Minister Honda's minority government. Its defining achievement was the passage of the [[National Universal Health Act]] in 2023. This landmark, and highly controversial, single-payer healthcare bill became the central political issue of the term, with the LDP opposition focusing on its high cost and associated tax increases.

=== 60th Diet (2025–Present) ===
The [[Okaiken general election, 2025|2025 general election]] resulted in a major political realignment. The PPO, while remaining the largest party, suffered a 20-seat loss. The LDP, under new leader [[Koichiro Sadanuma]], had a major resurgence, gaining 40 seats to become the clear Leader of the Opposition.

The most dramatic shift was the "Nakayama-mania" phenomenon, which saw the [[Okaiken Socialist Party|Okaiken Socialist Party (OSP)]] surge from 2 seats to 50 under its new 32-year-old leader, [[Rento Nakayama]]. The SCP, by contrast, lost half its seats. Lacking a majority, Prime Minister Honda formed a new coalition government between the PPO and the OSP (231 seats), appointing Nakayama as Minister of Health and Welfare.

== Election system ==
The 475 members of the House of Commoners are elected through a fictional system known as the '''Direct Party and Representative Voting System (DPR)''', which is a form of parallel voting. The 475 seats are divided into two groups, and each voter casts two ballots.

* '''Single-Member Constituencies:''' 295 members are elected from single-member districts under the first-past-the-post system. Voters cast a ballot for a local candidate.
* '''Proportional Blocks:''' 180 members are elected from 11 regional "proportional blocks." Voters cast a second ballot for a political party's list. Seats in each block are distributed proportionally to the parties based on the percentage of votes each party received in that block.

=== Right to vote and candidature ===
* Sanese nationals aged 18 years and older may vote.
* Sanese nationals aged 22 years and older may run for office in the House of Commoners.

